#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5050393b-0b84-58ad-8e86-fcb53e8add8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of Vertical Angles Given in Terms of a Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "d8d0babe-ca51-5d80-ba9c-5289697cb968" :question/math-academy-id "e-2579" :question/problem "Compute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d39f6b553856204eb1958c84fb06dc2756edb4e44beee4c55550aad8d3592f4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{15}^{∘} & =2x+{10}^{∘} \\\\ x-{15}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=25^\\circ,$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x + 10^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n2x + 10^\\circ = 2 \\cdot 25^\\circ + 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of $60^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fce9a0-b7fc-51b1-9335-f8853d0d8fe9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Angle by Combining Properties of Straight and Vertical Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "02660015-d4fd-53f3-b7a5-4a4118e406b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2583" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle. Determine $m \\angle AOD.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eac6c11dcc513cdf896cdfaa9650689af1bf58afd147783d0bb1ca61dd3a324.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle AOB$ is straight, $m\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC + m\\angle BOC = 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+{100}^{∘})+11x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 16x+{100}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 16x & ={80}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=5^\\circ,$ we can calculate $m \\angle COB$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\angle COB = 11(5^\\circ) = 55^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $\\angle AOD$ and $\\angle COB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOD = m \\angle COB = 55^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44a00376-bac8-5b09-baff-5dfd613c3222" :knowledge-point/title "Using Vertical Angles in a Situation With Three Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "a5dae4ad-b516-5ece-8d12-5805c5073fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2587" :question/problem "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ.$ Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4.$\n\n![](images/1b/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1=m∠2, \\\\ m∠3=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘}=m∠2, \\\\ {120}^{∘}=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠2+m∠4 & ={60}^{∘}+{120}^{∘} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6b7437-6922-58bd-a819-de7fb02a261e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity Using Vertical Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "11f90b96-0b4d-5f8d-8b0d-b4cde31fc7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3044" :question/problem "Given the figure below, find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b89eec86675b2bebfda74ecf1da8e0b7980e5de6d3a52de84e93e64a4a62068.png)" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. \nSince vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{12}^{∘} & ={54}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={42}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6b7437-6922-58bd-a819-de7fb02a261e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity Using Vertical Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a84106b-98b9-5583-8172-d48f0d697c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492304} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1e36ca-0303-51b3-88cd-367514f86088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492403} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6ea231-8b01-5d66-b52a-1286684dcb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492407} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebc881e-ea45-5ad8-b704-3719442338fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492435} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c2c631-12ac-543f-85fa-ce5542797494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ebc881e-ea45-5ad8-b704-3719442338fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492435} :answer-field/id #uuid "3187f453-97bb-56c2-93bd-6746899eb80e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cd0a1a3-7b23-5387-830e-7fe8d908d132" :question/math-academy-id "q-114804" :question/problem "![](images/50/5084bbe406dd4d44ffdeb6e485f6cf363780de097c9b483ba9f46a1a66b455a0.png)\n\nGiven the figure above, find the value of $w.$" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{3}+{30}^{∘} & ={46}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{w}{3} & ={16}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={48}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5050393b-0b84-58ad-8e86-fcb53e8add8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of Vertical Angles Given in Terms of a Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdcb6df-48c4-52ea-bdf7-9b4277eccfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492310} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdd8f09-ba9d-5df5-b763-3bb8adbf08ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492345} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5590db-b46d-5742-92ba-9cce38ee8869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492369} {:answer/id #uuid "702e16f9-92be-5088-ba74-cf3f9680ce57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492440} {:answer/id #uuid "a2538a7c-02a2-5964-81f0-1c7ccc207a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdcb6df-48c4-52ea-bdf7-9b4277eccfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492310} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d239139-54fa-55dc-952f-778d9d8bffd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19c18e1c-068f-53bc-9c01-193db0152518" :question/math-academy-id "q-114816" :question/problem "![](images/16/167f628be7f87d5fffa11fab9b9c8189880983a2364a992147e8e4bf84bbd13b.png)\n\nCompute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8w-{24}^{∘} & =6w-{6}^{∘} \\\\ 2w-{24}^{∘} & =-{6}^{∘} \\\\ 2w & ={18}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={9}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $w={9}^{∘},$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $6w-{6}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n6({9}^{∘})-{6}^{∘}={48}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of ${48}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fce9a0-b7fc-51b1-9335-f8853d0d8fe9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Angle by Combining Properties of Straight and Vertical Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "377f3a1b-5e07-541c-a94f-b1c513567dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{113}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492335} {:answer/id #uuid "66ee0543-c309-58a6-8132-2a4139cae805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{107}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492424} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1d42f6-9820-56bf-b10c-55ede29b6213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{95}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492471} {:answer/id #uuid "a760794f-75b5-525c-b70a-038d52cde7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{100}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492555} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd29a49-1e2c-5857-9c7d-fcea54e05c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{125}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ee0543-c309-58a6-8132-2a4139cae805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{107}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492424} :answer-field/id #uuid "be60585e-c426-51a1-bc8e-7cd292dae56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97e7e9d8-dcf7-50ae-a8f6-45c7e2519058" :question/math-academy-id "q-114829" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cafb9f7367b6f0f53a29e13e17f9b6c2cd6b516fa80afd1959842b52e176642c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠POQ$ is a straight angle. Find $m∠QOS.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠POQ$ is straight, $m∠POQ={180}^{∘}.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm∠QOR+m∠ROP={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4w-{7}^{∘})+(6w-{13}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10w-{20}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10w & ={200}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={20}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $w={20}^{∘},$ we can calculate $m∠ROP$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm∠ROP=6({20}^{∘})-{13}^{∘}={107}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $∠QOS$ and $∠ROP$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠QOS=m∠ROP={107}^{∘}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fce9a0-b7fc-51b1-9335-f8853d0d8fe9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Angle by Combining Properties of Straight and Vertical Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578b62f5-332b-5303-a97f-311f1eff667c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{115}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492383} {:answer/id #uuid "882325ab-a3ac-5ba7-8b01-0b170b1f0e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492486} {:answer/id #uuid "9f43dded-448e-5577-8e89-bd75aaa50359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{118}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492531} {:answer/id #uuid "c44a8416-dd42-5e16-be10-97a770987c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{95}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492607} {:answer/id #uuid "cc298b70-5db5-5c84-aa8d-ca401cc22d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc298b70-5db5-5c84-aa8d-ca401cc22d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{104}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492622} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f87137d-ca0a-5913-9d5e-eb858093c6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5703c4ee-d713-54f9-901a-aaa46a569aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-114834" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc5840723aad59a7632bc099e8f410df98234cf50f3532c0676f8bf96f673040.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠SOT$ is a straight angle. Find $m∠TOV.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠SOT$ is straight, $m∠SOT={180}^{∘}.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm∠SOU+m∠UOT={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2z+{60}^{∘})+(3z+{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5z+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5z & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={22}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $z={22}^{∘},$ we can calculate $m∠SOU$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm∠SOU=2({22}^{∘})+{60}^{∘}={104}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $∠SOU$ and $∠TOV$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠TOV=m∠SOU={104}^{∘}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44a00376-bac8-5b09-baff-5dfd613c3222" :knowledge-point/title "Using Vertical Angles in a Situation With Three Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ff897a6-0d24-528b-96b9-37b6a6132f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff897a6-0d24-528b-96b9-37b6a6132f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492410} :answer-field/id #uuid "159868c0-14b1-57b6-816a-3e596621b328" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d0960af-476c-5200-b5e5-db9a94a707b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d0960af-476c-5200-b5e5-db9a94a707b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492349} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d30f65f-f7bc-5ce0-af55-c5a1beb40838" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2571ba1e-66f6-5370-ac1a-d3c46be963c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-171786" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb9043c3082f373a92aaf6b8d4289e950d645eef7325cf5a3abc04ef83e31a7c.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={122}^{∘}$ and $m∠3={67}^{∘}.$ Then $m∠2=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}$ and $m∠4=$\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={122}^{∘}$ and $m∠3={67}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{122}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ {67}^{∘} & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6b7437-6922-58bd-a819-de7fb02a261e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity Using Vertical Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0feba6-10a5-52a9-a09b-083eeaf7ac6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492266} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8e3175-752e-537d-b8ff-86f5529a7875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492346} {:answer/id #uuid "73f4433e-9394-5dd7-9a7a-9e60b2990db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492449} {:answer/id #uuid "7e60b62d-6012-5194-80da-228802678bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{16}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492472} {:answer/id #uuid "f357346c-4884-5401-80de-a5f236e07ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a0feba6-10a5-52a9-a09b-083eeaf7ac6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7fce25f-fdf9-5f82-9a1c-59dc1108e26f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1962d5a7-c8a1-5f9e-a8c4-8c270c23c07a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26284" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5089419745362aa42e024c04df35dd1c68d61d79b0f1ae52c97afd4e76843b0.png)\n\nGiven the figure above, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{9}^{∘} & ={75}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={84}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={14}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44a00376-bac8-5b09-baff-5dfd613c3222" :knowledge-point/title "Using Vertical Angles in a Situation With Three Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fdcc23-9585-58da-90d0-ece5fce06da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492294} {:answer/id #uuid "80223d72-b965-5832-9820-d80f40ea2176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492474} {:answer/id #uuid "819895b7-079d-567f-bde9-93cf2b2972c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492478} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ac99a9-90ce-5068-af92-0cdb4162cb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492604} {:answer/id #uuid "f14d4356-27f8-58af-ad21-89505078c4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24fdcc23-9585-58da-90d0-ece5fce06da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492294} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41d4023-9f62-5631-9156-ee1b60a613dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f79072b2-718e-5891-9f14-a1fea25a8bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-26290" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d2a4e7a727bd5643187203bcfc30e4cc8369e1e1fe53eea4d11b50cfb405622.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={58}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={122}^{∘}.$ Determine the value of $m∠3-m∠2.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={58}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={122}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{58}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & ={122}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠3-m∠2 & ={122}^{∘}-{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={64}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44a00376-bac8-5b09-baff-5dfd613c3222" :knowledge-point/title "Using Vertical Angles in a Situation With Three Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110ff2f5-88f1-5823-bb7b-c612d3a1ff8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{176}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492247} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc1069-9183-5c8d-935b-5fff97e092d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{146}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492272} {:answer/id #uuid "4635fe22-0407-5309-8c9f-af59ddb32c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{190}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492355} {:answer/id #uuid "ade94a75-98a0-59ed-8a25-47900492f095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{126}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492565} {:answer/id #uuid "f46477e4-f220-5805-a013-9248858e57ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{192}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc1069-9183-5c8d-935b-5fff97e092d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{146}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492272} :answer-field/id #uuid "717cb0e3-7172-5bf0-8ea1-e1eda72c6ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b19a18fa-549e-563d-980d-10ad4fe9bdeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-26295" :question/problem "![](images/28/283adada8b7da0b7f481e0975a6b3b97c989e69fd63e0577839d00b6ee69a22b.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={35}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={111}^{∘}.$ Determine the value of $m∠2+m∠3.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={35}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={111}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{35}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & ={111}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠2+m∠3 & ={35}^{∘}+{111}^{∘} \\\\  & ={146}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5050393b-0b84-58ad-8e86-fcb53e8add8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of Vertical Angles Given in Terms of a Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149c69aa-e028-560f-8266-d809972c424b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492254} {:answer/id #uuid "2133f9d2-c935-5203-8542-5540f16f2828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492283} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea3652b-cdb3-5476-89f2-fe7577ee4139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492433} {:answer/id #uuid "a17fbd4a-7e1a-5e87-a6b7-3dc66dcadd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492538} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c31936-c89c-5fcc-9717-c9753724ebc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5c31936-c89c-5fcc-9717-c9753724ebc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492583} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6df7c54-8fba-571d-9d2a-8d68365d63ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "804e107f-83a7-522e-91c4-4c6cf836e13f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74745" :question/problem "![](images/ad/add18e4afb099c25b56b798ac69b8b02f7cee49949ef02b5971f71ffde0d7e5c.png)\n\nCompute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{32}^{∘} & =2x+{16}^{∘} \\\\ 4x-{32}^{∘} & ={16}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={48}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={12}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x={12}^{∘},$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x+{16}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n2({12}^{∘})+{16}^{∘}={40}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of ${40}^{∘}.$"}]}
